template_override": 1, "id": 11}], "stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n3le02ob03re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe8n3le02ob03re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> John:</span> I talked to the new programmer this morning, he told me about a conference he attended last month in Japan. He is planning to present the latest tendency in programming languages next week. He mentioned OOP. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Laura:</span> What do you mean by that? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> John:</span> It means object-oriented programming, that is, a programming model that divides the program responsibilities into objects and classes. Depending on the programming language you use, the syntax and semantics can change. However, it focuses on the objects you want to operate rather than the logic the program requires to manipulate them. You can perceive this model as a logical procedure that takes input data, processes it, and produces output data. This model reduces development time and ensures more accuracy while coding."}, {"time": "50000", "image": "enpe8n3le02ob03re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Laura:</span> I get it. I had to read about OOP's history for a presentation last week; it originated as the dominant programming methodology in the early 60's and became popular in the mid-1990s. I read that the Xerox PARC laboratory implemented object-oriented programming to create their Smalltalk software. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> John:</span> That's right! They used OOP to design Smalltalk as well as Microsoft implemented C++ to relate the classes and objects within their programs.\nThe programmer also told me about some of the different languages that companies implemented long ago and the in-demand ones that are trend nowadays; perhaps you used them or heard of them. For instance, Python, C, C++, C#, Java, Ruby, Delphi, or PHP."}, {"time": "101000", "image": "enpe8n3le02ob03re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Laura:</span> I heard many of them were really common back in the day, like C, but now the ones that people implement the most to create, for example, websites or apps are JavaScript, Java, and C#. For example, we had to use Java to create applications on android and JavaScript to make a dynamic website for the company last time."}], "coverimg": "enpe8n3le02ob03re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "type": "scholar", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "id": 0}]}